From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:37:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: nn::ssl over SecureTransport, on the game's own socket
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every nn::ssl name the games import was already a stub in hle_net.c, so this gives the ones
that need a connection a body instead of guessing names. The TLS stack is the platform's —
SecureTransport, deprecated since iOS 13 but present in every SDK, and the only synchronous one
(the alternative, Network.framework, is async and every nn::ssl call here blocks).

The handshake and the traffic run through SSLSetIOFuncs over dm_sk_recvfrom/dm_sk_sendto, so
the guest's blocking or non-blocking mode, its timeouts and its errno stay in hle_net.c, and a
socket the game created with nn::socket is all it takes. Verification is the default; a game
that asks for none gets kSSLSessionOptionBreakOnServerAuth, which is how iOS spells it.

With DM_NEXTENDO unset every entry answers what the stub answered before: a context is created
and destroyed, a connection's calls fail, Read and Write return -1. -framework Security joined
HOST_FRAMEWORKS, which is where the iOS project takes its frameworks from.

The handshake is compiled (three host profiles, iOS SDK) but not yet exercised end to end: the
test that speaks to a local TLS server is the next step.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: upstream cuts nn::ssl outright (hle_offline.c); a Nextendo client needs TLS for the auth connection and the dauth/account calls, and the fork keeps it behind DM_NEXTENDO
Issue: none opened yet — notes/nextendo-spike.md carries the plan and the open question for Nextendo

